.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt OPENSSL-PASSPHRASE-OPTIONS 1ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm openssl-passphrase-options
.Nd Pass phrase options
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm openssl
.Em command
.Oo
.Em options
\&...
.Oc
.Oo
.Em parameters
\&...
.Oc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Several OpenSSL commands accept password arguments, typically using
.Sy -passin
and
.Sy -passout
for input and output passwords respectively.
These allow the password to be obtained from a variety of sources.
Both of these options take a single argument whose format is described
below.
If no password argument is given and a password is required then the
user is prompted to enter one: this will typically be read from the
current terminal with echoing turned off.
.Pp
Note that character encoding may be relevant, please see
.Xr passphrase-encoding 7 .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Ss Pass Phrase Option Arguments
Pass phrase arguments can be formatted as follows.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Sy pass: Ns Em password
The actual password is
.Em password .
Since the password is visible to utilities (like 'ps' under Unix) this
form should only be used where security is not important.
.It Sy env: Ns Em var
Obtain the password from the environment variable
.Em var .
Since the environment of other processes is visible on certain platforms
(e.g.
ps under certain Unix OSes) this option should be used with caution.
.It Sy file: Ns Em pathname
Reads the password from the specified file
.Em pathname ,
which can be a regular file, device, or named pipe.
Only the first line, up to the newline character, is read from the
stream.
.Pp
If the same
.Em pathname
argument is supplied to both
.Sy -passin
and
.Sy -passout
arguments, the first line will be used for the input password, and the
next line will be used for the output password.
.It Sy fd: Ns Em number
Reads the password from the file descriptor
.Em number .
This can be useful for sending data via a pipe, for example.
The same line handling as described for
.Sy file:
applies to passwords read from file descriptors.
.Pp
.Sy fd:
is not supported on Windows.
.It Sy stdin
Reads the password from standard input.
The same line handling as described for
.Sy file:
applies to passwords read from standard input.
.El
